1
00:06:05,454 --> 00:06:11,454
I like to remember things my own way. Not necessarily the way they happened.

2
00:12:10,909 --> 00:12:16,909
Lost Highway is a drama, thriller, mystery film directed by David Lynch.

3
00:18:16,363 --> 00:18:22,363
A tormented jazz musician finds himself lost in an enigmatic story involving murder, surveillance,
gangsters, doppelgängers, and an impossible transformation inside a prison cell.

4
00:24:21,818 --> 00:24:27,818
Renee Madison / Alice Wakefield, played by Patricia Arquette.

5
00:30:27,272 --> 00:30:33,272
Fred Madison, played by Bill Pullman.

6
00:36:32,727 --> 00:36:38,727
Pete Dayton, played by Balthazar Getty.

7
00:42:38,181 --> 00:42:44,181
Mystery Man, played by Robert Blake.

8
00:48:43,636 --> 00:48:49,636
Mr. Eddy / Dick Laurent, played by Robert Loggia.

9
00:54:49,090 --> 00:54:55,090
Andy, played by Michael Massee.

10
01:00:54,545 --> 01:01:00,545
Lost Highway is a 1997 surrealist neo-noir horror film directed by
David Lynch, who co-wrote it with Barry Gifford.

11
01:07:00,000 --> 01:07:06,000
The film stars Bill Pullman, Patricia Arquette, and Balthazar Getty, as well as
Robert Blake, Jack Nance, and Richard Pryor in their final film roles.

12
01:13:05,454 --> 01:13:11,454
It follows a man who receives unmarked VHS tapes showing footage of his home before he is abruptly arrested for his wife's
murder, at which point he mysteriously disappears and is replaced by a young man leading a different life.

13
01:19:10,909 --> 01:19:16,909
Financed by French production company Ciby 2000 and Lynch's own Asymmetrical Productions, the film was largely shot in Los
Angeles, where Lynch collaborated with cinematographer Peter Deming and frequent producer and editor Mary Sweeney.

14
01:25:16,363 --> 01:25:22,363
The film's surreal narrative structure has been likened to a Möbius strip, while Lynch has
described it as a "psychogenic fugue" rather than a conventionally logical story.

15
01:31:21,818 --> 01:31:27,818
The film's soundtrack was produced by Nine Inch Nails frontman Trent Reznor, featuring an original score by Angelo Badalamenti and Barry
Adamson alongside selected songs by Nine Inch Nails, David Bowie, Marilyn Manson, Rammstein, and the Smashing Pumpkins.

16
01:37:27,272 --> 01:37:33,272
Lost Highway received mixed reviews upon release, with most critics initially dismissing it as
incoherent; it has since been reappraised, garnering a cult following and scholarly interest.

17
01:43:32,727 --> 01:43:38,727
It was a box office failure, grossing $3.8 million on a budget
of $15 million after a modest three-week run.

18
01:49:38,181 --> 01:49:44,181
It is the first of three Lynch films set in Los Angeles, followed by
Mulholland Drive (2001) and his final film Inland Empire (2006).

19
01:55:43,636 --> 01:55:49,636
The film was adapted into an opera by Austrian composer Olga Neuwirth in 2003.

20
02:01:49,090 --> 02:01:55,090
The film explores themes of prison, pornography,
jealousy, dreams, dual identity.

21
02:07:54,545 --> 02:08:00,545
The tone is intense and dark and emotional.
